WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.680
For as long as humans
have looked at the stars,

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.280
we have wondered how
they got there -

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:05.800
and what lies beyond them.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.440
Scientists have long searched
for a simple theory

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.600
that explains how
the universe works,

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:13.840
a theory of everything.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.360
Many theories have been tested

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.560
and so far, every one of them
has failed to fully explain

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.240
what we see in our universe.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.880
But there is one
particularly enticing idea

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:27.640
that some theoretical physicists
think might just be ‘it’,

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:28.880
String Theory.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:31.120
String theory is finite.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.680
It doesn't blow up.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.000
It doesn't collapse on itself.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:36.480
That's why we believe in it.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:40.280
Other theories do collapse.
Other theories do blow up.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:41.560
But string theory does not.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.760
Dr Michio Kaku has spent decades

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.000
grappling with -
and attempting to answer -

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.760
some of life’s biggest questions.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:56.840
What happened before the Big Bang?

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.600
Are there other universes?

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.760
What lies on the other side
of creation?

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.520
The other side of a black hole?

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:07.640
Are gateways possible?

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.720
Wormholes? Higher dimensions?

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.400
Do we live in a multiverse?

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.400
All these questions
cannot be answered

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:16.400
using our present-day understanding.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.120
When Dr Kaku talks about our
present-day understanding,

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.080
he means our current best theories
about the way the universe works.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:27.120
In truth, they contradict each other.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.800
And sometimes even
give conflicting results -

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:34.840
Albert Einstein’s general theory
of relativity works perfectly well

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.120
for predictions about the
movements of stars and galaxies,

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:42.400
but doesn’t work when applied to the
behaviour of subatomic particles.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:46.520
Conversely, quantum theory is great
with atoms

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:50.080
but predicts the whole universe
should collapse into a black hole,

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.640
which it clearly doesn't
because we’re all still here!

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.080
So, will we ever find a single
theory that explains everything?

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.680
Two thousand years ago, Pythagoras
asked himself that question.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:05.760
The great Greek mathematician

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.280
said that there must
be a unifying principle,

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.680
a paradigm by which to summarise
the vast creation

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.040
that we see all around us
of the universe that we know.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.520
And he looked around
and saw a lyra string.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.040
You pluck a lyra string and
you get a note,

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.440
you pluck another one,
you get another note.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:31.640
And then he said, aha, the
mathematics of music is rich enough

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:35.160
to explain the diversity of
everything we see around us.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:39.400
And only recently have we
come up with a new idea

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.320
based on the Pythagorean
idea of music.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.840
In other words, string theory.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:49.680
So how do we go from Pythagoras’
musical musings to proper physics?

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:53.240
The first port of call would be a
particle accelerator

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:56.120
like the Large Hadron Collider
at CERN in Switzerland,

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:00.800
where tiny particles are smashed
to pieces in high energy collisions

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.720
and then closely studied.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:05.720
These experiments are our
best way of testing theories

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:07.680
about how the universe works.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:09.880
So what does string theory
predict we would see?

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:12.360
We now believe that all these
subatomic particles,

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:14.280
hundreds of subatomic particles

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.800
that we get by smashing protons
at the Large Hadron Collider,

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:22.880
they're nothing but musical notes
as Pythagoras believed.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:25.080
If I had a super microscope

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:26.920
and I could peer into an electron,

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.400
what would I see?

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:30.840
I would see a rubber band,

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.120
a vibrating rubber band.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.400
Of course, Dr Kaku doesn’t mean
an actual rubber band.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.560
But rather something
rubber-band-like.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:42.520
Or to put it another way -
the strings in string theory.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:45.360
And just like the strings
on a musical instrument,

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:48.280
if you put some energy
into them, they vibrate.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.920
It vibrates one way,
we call it an electron.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:54.200
We vibrate it another way,
it's called a neutrino.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:56.960
We vibrate it another way,
it's called a quark.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.520
But it's the same rubber band.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.280
So, string theory offers
a tantalising possibility -

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:07.360
an explanation for the vast variety
we see in the universe,

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.200
from the collisions of stars
to the collisions of atoms.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:14.000
Of course, string theory is just that
- a theory.

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:17.880
Its critics point out that many of
its predictions are untestable -

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:20.560
something Dr Kaku himself
acknowledges.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.000
Its proponents though,
consider it to be

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:25.720
the best hope of unifying physics.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.320
Dr Kaku believes string theory

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:30.120
could even explain
the mystery that is dark matter.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:34.840
Dark matter makes up most
of the matter of the universe.

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:40.280
It is invisible and it holds
the galaxies together,

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:42.360
but how do we prove it?

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:47.320
We think that dark matter could be
the next octave of the string.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:51.000
If you could magnify all the
particles you see around us,

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:53.120
we would see a lot of rubber bands,

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:56.640
a lot of rubber bands
vibrating at different frequencies.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:00.080
But the rubber band
has higher octaves.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:02.800
That, we think, is dark matter.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:07.800
If Dr Kaku is right, the huge
complexity of the entire universe

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:12.000
could be reduced to the simple
and elegant vibration of strings.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.440
I think the one thing
that people should realise

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:17.840
is that physics,
at the fundamental level,

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:22.440
gets simpler and simpler
the deeper we go,

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:25.600
but becomes more powerful,
the deeper we go.

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:28.800
The universe is simpler
than we thought.

